WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.550
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:09.160
In the previous episode, we talked about image
classification, which means that we have an

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:14.969
image as an input, and we ask a computer to
figure out what is seen in this image.

00:00:14.969 --> 00:00:20.800
Learning algorithms, such as convolutional
neural networks are amazing at it, however,

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:26.250
we just found out that even though their results
are excellent, it is still quite hard to find

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:31.500
out how they get to make a decision that an
image depicts a dog or cat.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:37.079
This is in contrast to and old and simple
technique that goes by the name bag of words.

00:00:37.079 --> 00:00:42.390
It works a bit like looking for keywords in
a document and by using those, trying to find

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.660
out what the writing is about.

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:49.090
Kind of like the shortcut students like to
take for mandatory readings.

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:50.530
We have all done it.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:56.660
Now, imagine the same for images, where we
slice up the image into small pieces and keep

00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:59.719
a score on what it seen in these snippets.

00:00:59.719 --> 00:01:06.310
Floppy ears, black snout, fur, okay, we’re
good, we can conclude that we have a dog over

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:07.310
here.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:12.780
But wait, I hear you are saying, Károly,
why do we need to digress from AI to bag of

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:13.900
words?

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:15.870
Why talk about this old method?

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:21.490
Well, let’s look at the advantages and disadvantages
and you will see in a moment.

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:26.460
The advantage of bag of features is that it
is quite easy to interpret because it is an

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.720
open book: it gives us the scores for all
of these small snippets.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.060
We know exactly how a decision is being made.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:39.689
A disadvantage, one would say is that because
it works per snippet, it ignores the bigger

00:01:39.689 --> 00:01:46.229
spatial relationships in an image, and therefore
overall, it must be vastly inferior to a neural

00:01:46.229 --> 00:01:47.860
network, right?

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:51.700
Well, let’s set up an experiment and see!

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:56.719
This is a paper from the same group as the
previous episode at the University of Tübingen.

00:01:56.719 --> 00:02:02.200
The experiment works the following way: let’s
try to combine bag of words with neural networks

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:08.300
by slicing up the image into the same patches,
and then, feed them into a neural network

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.670
and ask it to classify them.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:16.070
In this case, the neural network will do many
small classification tasks on image snippets

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:19.650
instead of one big decision for the full image.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:25.780
The paper discusses that the final classification
also involves evaluating heatmaps and more.

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:31.300
This way, we are hoping that we get a technique
where a neural network would explain its decisions,

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:34.170
much like how bag of words works.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:37.520
For now, let’s call these networks BagNets.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.220
And now, hold on to your papers, because results
are really surprising.

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:48.030
As expected, it is true that looking at small
snippets of the image can lead to misunderstandings.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:53.140
For instance, this image contains a soccer
ball, but when zooming into small patches,

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:58.460
it might seem like this is a cowboy hat on
top of the head of this child.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:04.500
However, what is unexpected is that even with
this, BagNet produces surprisingly similar

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:09.840
results to a state-of-the-art neural network
by the name ResNet.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.780
This is…wow.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:13.340
This has several corollaries.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:18.590
Let’s start with the cool one: this means
that neural networks are great at identifying

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:22.390
objects in scrambled images, but humans are
not.

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:26.650
The reason for that is that the order of the
tiles don’t really matter.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:31.600
We now have a better reason why this is the
case — doing all this classification for

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:38.330
many small tiles independently has superpowers
when it comes to processing scrambled images.

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:43.640
The other, more controversial corollary is
that this inevitably means that some results

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:49.750
that show the superiority of deep neural networks
over the good old bag of features come not

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:55.110
from using a superior method, but from careful
fine-tuning.

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:58.080
Not all results, …some results.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:03.800
As always, a good piece of research challenges
our underlying assumptions, and sometimes,

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:05.870
in this case, even our sanity.

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:10.620
There is a lot to say about this topic and
we have only scratched the surface, so take

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:15.570
this as a thought-provoking idea that is worthy
of further discussion.

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:17.750
Really cool work, I love it.

